INSERT INTO `settings` (`id`, `api_key`, `openai_api_key`, `model`, `tone`, `custom_prompt`, `target_audience`, `brand`, `word_count_mode`, `word_count_divisor`, `target_word_count`, `target_language`, `cta_link`, `wp_url`, `wp_username`, `wp_app_password`, `updated_at`) VALUES (1,'','','gpt-5.4-mini','Authoritative','**Structural Requirements (The Poly-Intent Framework)**\n\n**1. Global Architecture**\n\n- **\"Atomic\" Structure:** Every section and paragraph must make sense in isolation. Do NOT use dependent transition clauses like \"As mentioned above\" or \"As we saw earlier,\" as these break context when extracted by AI.\n- **Inverted Pyramid:** Place the most critical keywords and the direct answer to the user\'s primary intent in the first 50-70 words to combat the \"Lost in the Middle\" effect.\n- **Reading Level:** Grade 8-10. Use active voice and simple sentence structures for maximum \"fluency\" and machine readability.\n\n**2. Section-by-Section Instructions**\n\n- **A. The Header (SEO Layer):**\n    - **H1 Tag:** Must contain the Primary Keyword.\n    - **TL;DR Summary:** Immediately below the H1, provide a \"Too Long; Didn\'t Read\" bulleted box. This acts as a meta-abstract packed with primary entities and keywords.\n- **B. The Introduction (The 50-Word Critical Zone):**\n    - **Front-Load Intent:** Address the user\'s core intent immediately.\n    - **Proof of Humanity:** Use a first-person perspective (\"We tested,\" \"Our local guides analyzed\") to establish E-E-A-T and differentiate from generic AI content.\n- **C. The Body (AEO & GEO Layers):**\n    - **Interrogative Headers:** Structure H2s and H3s as natural language questions (Who, What, Where, When, How).\n    - **\"Answer-First\" Protocol:** Immediately after every Question Header, provide a 40–60 word concise definition using declarative sentences for \"Position Zero\" extraction.\n    - **Semantic Proximity:** Ensure related niche keywords appear physically close to each other (e.g., \"Mount Bromo,\" \"golden hour,\" \"wide-angle lens,\" \"caldera\") to aid vector embeddings.\n    - **Conversational Transitions:** Use logical connectors (\"Furthermore,\" \"In contrast,\" \"Specifically regarding\") to improve fluency.\n- **D. Citation Worthiness (GEO Layer):**\n    - **Information Gain:** Include (or simulate based on facts) \"high-fidelity data points\" (e.g., \"68% of travelers\").\n    - **Data Tables:** Include at least one markdown table comparing features, travel seasons, gear, or pros/cons.\n    - **Counter-Narrative:** Include at least one nuanced or contrarian insight (a \"Yes, but...\" perspective) that challenges common assumptions about the topic to increase information gain.\n\n**3. Outcome-Driven Calls to Action (CTAs)**\nSeamlessly integrate three distinct CTAs throughout the article. The copy for these CTAs MUST focus purely on communicating the *outcome, value, and emotional payoff* the user will experience by clicking, rather than the mechanical action itself. also, we focused on long day tour so the copy offers is not limited by one destination, even if the article is only talk about one destination.\n\n1. **Conversion CTA (Free Consultation/Trip Planning):** Positioned near the middle or end. Focus on the outcome of having a stress-free, expertly crafted itinerary. *(e.g., \"Map out your perfect Indonesian photography itinerary with a free, no-obligation route consultation.\")*\n\n**4. Formatting & Technical Rules (Strict Adherence Required)**\n\n- **Continuous Paragraphs:** Write paragraphs as standard, continuous blocks of text. Do NOT add line breaks, hard returns, or bullet points between sentences within the same paragraph.\n- **Invisible Architecture:** Do NOT print structural labels, meta-tags, or internal prompt instructions in the final text. Never include words like \"[Answer-First Protocol]\", \"Section C\", or \"CTA 1\" in the output. Output only the clean, reader-facing content and standard Markdown headings (`##`, `###`).\n- **Lists:** Use bullet points frequently for parsability.\n- **Tone:** Objective, professional, and authoritative. Avoid cheap, sales-heavy language.\n- **Inclusive Language:** Strictly use inclusive, respectful, empathetic, and gender-neutral language (e.g., \'travelers\', \'photographers\', \'guests\', \'they/them\'). Avoid gender-biased, ableist, ageist, or culturally insensitive terms.\n- **Technical Terminology:** Use precise domain-specific jargon for photography and travel (e.g., \"exposure triangle,\" \"focal length,\" \"topography\").\n- **FAQs:** Plan an FAQ section utilizing \"People Also Ask\" concepts.\n- **Sentence Length:** Strictly limit sentences to a maximum of 20 words to maximize AI parsing fluency.\n- **Paragraph Length:** Maximum 3-4 sentences (approx. 40-50 words) per paragraph.','High-net-worth individuals from Europe, the US, Australia, and Dubai interested in travel and photography tours in Indonesia.','Panorama Lens Trip (Providing specialized photography tours package and premium long day tours around Indonesia).','total',10,2500,'English','https://wa.me/+6282132838229?text=Hello+Panorama+Lens+Trip%21','https://panoramalenstrip.com','admin@your-domain.com','xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx','2026-09-22 06:39:10');
